What NVIDIA's nvbootctrl of Jetson Linux r39.2.1 (nvidia-l4t-bootloader-utils
39.2.1-20260806224157) prints for `nvbootctrl -t rootfs dump-slots-info`, for
test/unit/tegra-rootfs-ab.bats. Nobody has recorded these on a module yet
(WP-6.6). They are written by hand from the tool's format strings
(`Current rootfs slot: %s`, `Active rootfs slot: %s`, `num_slots: %d`,
`slot: %d,             retry_count: %d,             status: %s` with
`normal` or `unbootable`, `RootFS A/B is not enabled.`), and the slot letters
as NVIDIA's own nv-l4t-bootloader-update.sh reads them from the bootloader's
lines (`awk '{print $4}'` gives A or B). Nothing here is NVIDIA's.

- dump-slots-info.flashed: a module just flashed with ROOTFS_AB=1; slot A
  runs, both slots have their three tries.
- dump-slots-info.fallen-back: the firmware has given slot B up after three
  boots of it and runs slot A again, which has used one try since.
- dump-slots-info.ab-disabled: a module flashed without rootfs A/B.

fake-nvbootctrl answers the commands the backend uses from a model of the
module (fake-nvbootctrl's header) and prints the same format. For
tegra-capsule (WP-6.5) it also prints the bootloader's dump-slots-info, from
the format strings `Current bootloader slot: %s`, `Active bootloader slot:
%s`, `num_slots: 2` and `slot: %d,             status: %s`; which status
words other than `normal` the tool prints is not known, and tegra-capsule
treats every other word as a failed chain.
